From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Stephen Bates <sbates@raithlin.com>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 18:56:59 -0500
Subject: [RFC PATCH not-for-net-next 5/6] ionic: don't send an unreadable skb that has no frags

Not for net-next, where commit 23fe6ac2e97a ("net: skbuff: make
skb_copy_and_csum_bits() report unreadable frags") fixes this in the
core. This is for trees without that commit: v7.4-rc3, which this series
is based on for now, and backports.

The payload of an skb with unreadable frags (a devmem skb) is only in
those frags: it can't be copied. So an skb that is marked unreadable and
has no frags does not carry what its sender put in it.

Without the commit above the stack makes such skbs. With NETIF_F_SG and
NETIF_F_HW_CSUM both off, software GSO of an unreadable skb takes the
copy path of skb_segment(), where skb_copy_and_csum_bits() fills each
segment with zeroes and reports no error. The segments get a valid
checksum from tcp_gso_segment() and keep the unreadable mark, which
__copy_skb_header() copies. validate_xmit_skb() lets them through to the
driver, and the peer receives zeroes as data. On a device that has
netmem_tx set, "ethtool -K $dev sg off tx off" under a devmem TCP
connection that is already established gets there.

Following the features with netmem_tx doesn't close this in a driver:
qdiscs that segment (cake, tbf, taprio, netem, dualpi2) do it at enqueue
with the features of that moment, so the segments can be sent after the
features changed again. And every driver sets netmem_tx once, before
the netdev is registered; the xmit path reads it with no lock.

Drop these skbs in ionic_tx_descs_needed(), which both xmit functions
call first. They are counted in tx_unreadable_drop.

Derived from the source; I have not seen the zeroes on the wire.
---
 drivers/net/ethernet/pensando/ionic/ionic_txrx.c | 9 +++++++++
 1 file changed, 9 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/net/ethernet/pensando/ionic/ionic_txrx.c b/drivers/net/ethernet/pensando/ionic/ionic_txrx.c
index 5a06fde24992..22a4eab750d3 100644
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/pensando/ionic/ionic_txrx.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/pensando/ionic/ionic_txrx.c
@@ -1715,6 +1715,15 @@ static int ionic_tx_descs_needed(struct ionic_queue *q, struct sk_buff *skb)
 	int ndescs;
 	int err;
 
+	/* The payload of an unreadable skb is in its frags, so one without
+	 * frags is not what its sender made.  The stack makes them when it
+	 * segments an unreadable GSO skb for a device with NETIF_F_SG and
+	 * NETIF_F_HW_CSUM off: it can't copy the payload and puts zeroes in
+	 * its place.  Don't send those.
+	 */
+	if (unlikely(!skb_frags_readable(skb) && !nr_frags))
+		return -EFAULT;
+
 	/* Each desc is mss long max, so a descriptor for each gso_seg */
 	if (skb_is_gso(skb)) {
 		ndescs = skb_shinfo(skb)->gso_segs;
